{"actor":{"id":"deepseek-v4-flash","type":"agent"},"content":{"agent":"explore","blocks":[{"id":"call_58307305508fb4b01059f162b72776dc","input":{"filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md"},"name":"read","type":"tool_use"},{"content":"<path>/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Conversation Ledger\n2: \n3: Durable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4: your repository as git notes.\n5: \n6: `cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7: utility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8: `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9: conversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10: conversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11: squash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12: ride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13: pre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14: \n15: Conversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16: \n17: - `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18: - `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19: - `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20: \n21: The ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22: visibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23: \n24: ## Install\n25: \n26: ```sh\n27: git clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28: cd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29: cledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n30: ```\n31: \n32: `cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n33: and a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n34: backing both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n35: repository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n36: idempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n37: duplicates.\n38: \n39: Two one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n40: capture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n41: interactively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n42: codex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n43: <source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n44: \n45: The hooks above are installed once, globally, and fire in every git\n46: repository you work in. To turn cledger off for one specific repo, add\n47: `{\"enabled\": false}` to that repo's `.cledger.json`: hook capture, manual\n48: `cledger append`, and backfill all become no-ops, before any ledger read or\n49: write happens. Existing history is untouched and still readable via\n50: `cledger log`/`show`/`export` — this only stops new events from being\n51: written. Outside any git repository, hooks are already a silent no-op, so\n52: there's nothing to record and nothing to disable.\n53: \n54: ## Use\n55: \n56: ```sh\n57: cledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n58: cledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n59: cledger log --model gpt-5.6-sol # only turns a given model served\n60: cledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch, with the models they used\n61: cledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n62: cledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n63: cledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n64: cledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n65: echo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n66: ```\n67: \n68: ## Sharing (transport)\n69: \n70: The ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n71: \n72: - **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n73:   safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n74:   `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n75:   ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n76:   ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n77:   `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n78: - **Push is branch-scoped** (0.14.0): only conversations reachable from the\n79:   refs you're pushing are shared, so an abandoned or unmerged branch's\n80:   conversations stay on your machine. Squash-merged work still rides along —\n81:   the scope follows `re_anchor` mappings the same way reads do.\n82:   `cledger sync --all` pushes the whole ledger; `--rev R` scopes elsewhere.\n83: - **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n84:   `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n85:   (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n86:   `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n87: - **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n88:   `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n89:   marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n90:   explicitly either way.\n91: \n92: If git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n93: are recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n94: the same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n95: and never guesses a hostname-based one.\n96: \n97: ## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n98: \n99: Events anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n100: merge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n101: branch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n102: the default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n103: \n104: - After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n105:   your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n106:   exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n107:   cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n108:   event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n109:   branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n110:   never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n111:   and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n112: - Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n113:   the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n114:   (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n115:   candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n116:   squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n117:   touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n118:   and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n119:   conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n120:   `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n121:   mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n122:   commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n123:   explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n124:   evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n125:   turns them off.\n126: - Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n127:   working either way.\n128: \n129: ## Adapters\n130: \n131: Supported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n132: \n133: Every captured event records the agent that served it — `producer.model`,\n134: `producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — but only where the source\n135: states it; see the Roadmap entry below for what each adapter does and does\n136: not know.\n137: \n138: | Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n139: |---|---|---|---|\n140: | Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n141: | Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Provider-encrypted `reasoning` items are preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events (hidden from `log`/`show` by default, see Roadmap); inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text but their embedded encrypted blocks are still dropped outright. |\n142: \n143: TODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n144: story looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n145: systems):\n146: \n147: - **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n148: - **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n149: - **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n150: - **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n151: - **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n152: - **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n153: - **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n154: - **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n155: - **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n156: \n157: ## Security & redaction\n158: \n159: Visible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n160: \n161: ### Redaction layers\n162: \n163: **Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n164: - Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n165: - Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n166: \n167: **Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n168: - Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n169: - Cost: manual configuration required.\n170: \n171: **Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n172: - Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n173: - Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n174: \n175: **Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n176: - Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n177: - Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n178: \n179: **Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n180: - Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n181: - Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n182: - Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n183: \n184: ### Commands\n185: \n186: `cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n187: \n188: `cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n189: \n190: ### Maximum safety recipe\n191: \n192: Keep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n193: \n194: ## Roadmap\n195: \n196: - **Config sections replace rather than merge, and it fails open** — `loadConfig`\n197:   merges `~/.config/cledger/config.json` and `<repo>/.cledger.json` per\n198:   *top-level section*, not per key (`const redact = override.redact ??\n199:   base.redact`). So a repo that commits a `.cledger.json` with *any* `redact`\n200:   key — a `patterns` list, say — silently replaces the user's entire `redact`\n201:   section, and a globally-enabled `redact.knownSecrets: true` reverts to its\n202:   default of `false`. Capture-time scrubbing of learned secret values then\n203:   stops in that repo, with nothing printed and nothing in the ledger to show\n204:   it happened. Same shape as the pre-0.13.0 worktree bug: silent, fails open,\n205:   visible only if you go looking. Fix is a deep merge within each section, or\n206:   at minimum a warning when a repo config overrides a section the user config\n207:   had already set. Worth doing together with a general sweep for this class.\n208: \n209:   **Self-referential failures are now this project's characteristic bug\n210:   family**, and are worth a standing check rather than four independent\n211:   fixes. cledger records the work of building cledger, so its own state,\n212:   output, and documentation feed back into its behavior. Known members: the\n213:   capture/scan redaction loop (a finding report re-seeding the finding it\n214:   describes — closed in 0.6.1/0.7.0); the pre-0.13.0 worktree state split\n215:   (0.13.0); this config section-replace; and the allowlist entry below, whose\n216:   *write-up* of a false positive reproduces the false positive in every repo\n217:   whose conversations quote it. Three of the four fail silently, and two fail\n218:   open. A useful tripwire when adding anything that reads cledger's own\n219:   state or prints cledger's own findings: ask what happens when that output\n220:   is itself captured.\n221: - **Don't print findings by default; print a pointer to them** — the cleanest\n222:   structural break in the self-referential loop above. Today `cledger scan`\n223:   and the sync gate print every finding inline, which is exactly the text that\n224:   gets captured into the next conversation and re-seeds the finding. Masking\n225:   the matched span (0.6.1) removed the secret characters but not the\n226:   *surrounding* credential-shaped context, which is what\n227:   `keyword-assignment` trips on — so the loop is narrowed, not closed.\n228:   Proposed: report only the count and how to inspect, with the audience split\n229:   made explicit in the text — a human is told which command reveals the\n230:   findings; an agent is told not to run it, because reading the findings into\n231:   its context is what reproduces them, and to surface the count to its human\n232:   instead. Naming the failure mode in the output is the point: an agent that\n233:   understands *why* will comply, where a bare \"don't look\" invites a\n234:   workaround. Pairs naturally with the inspection command in the design doc's\n235:   open questions, which already has to write unmasked output to a file rather\n236:   than stdout for the same reason. *Tension to resolve first:* CI reads those\n237:   printed findings, and `cledger scan`'s nonzero exit plus inline report is\n238:   the documented CI integration — so the default likely has to be\n239:   terminal-aware, or CI has to opt in explicitly (`--report`), rather than\n240:   simply going quiet for everyone.\n241: - **Branch-scoped push** *(shipped, 0.14.0)* — reachability filtered *reads*\n242:   (`cledger log`, `show`, `conversations`), but the notes ref is a single ref\n243:   and a ref push sends all of it, so conversations from a branch that was\n244:   abandoned and never merged still reached `origin` and landed on every\n245:   collaborator's disk. Two scopes wearing one name, with push the odd one out:\n246:   storage was already per-commit and reads already per-branch.\n247: \n248:   Push now carries only the anchors a read at that rev resolves to. Storage is\n249:   unchanged (commit-anchored, one local ref, one shared remote ref) — only\n250:   push learns the granularity storage already had. `cledger sync` scopes to\n251:   the current branch by default, `--rev` scopes elsewhere, `--all` restores\n252:   the whole-ledger push. **No mapping is stored anywhere:** reachability is\n253:   recomputed at push time from the DAG, the same reason reads never stored it.\n254: \n255:   *Squash merges are the case that decides the implementation.* The scope\n256:   reuses `resolveAnchors` — the same function reads use — rather than a plain\n257:   `git rev-list`. A squash discards the source branch's commits, so its\n258:   conversations sit on anchors unreachable from the target, and only the\n259:   `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the *surviving* commit) tie them\n260:   back. A rev-list-only scope would push those mapping events while leaving\n261:   behind the events they point at — dropping exactly what re-anchoring exists\n262:   to save. Covered by a test that fails against a rev-list-only scope.\n263: \n264:   *The destructive shortcut, recorded because it is the natural first\n265:   implementation.* A notes ref is a snapshot of the whole database, not an\n266:   append log: its tip tree maps every annotated commit to its note, so\n267:   whatever tree you push becomes the remote's entire notes database. Filtering\n268:   your local ref down to one branch and pushing that still fast-forwards — and\n269:   silently deletes every other branch's conversations from the remote's\n270:   current view (they survive in the ref's history but vanish from `git notes\n271:   show` and from any fresh clone). The scoped push therefore seeds a temp ref\n272:   from the remote's tip and unions its scope *into* that, so the push strictly\n273:   adds. Also covered by a test that fails against the filtered-tree version.\n274: \n275:   *Side benefit:* the pre-push scan gate now inspects only what the push would\n276:   carry, so a finding in a branch that is not being pushed no longer blocks\n277:   shipping unrelated work.\n278: \n279:   *Scope follows the refs actually being pushed* (0.15.0). The hook is fed\n280:   git's pre-push stdin — one line per ref — so `git push origin feat` from\n281:   another branch shares feat's conversations. 0.14.0 shipped with `</dev/null`\n282:   and scoped by `HEAD`, which shared nothing in that case. Ref deletions are\n283:   skipped (no tip to scope to), multiple refs union, and anything unusable\n284:   falls back to `HEAD` rather than to the whole ledger. Because this changes\n285:   the installed hook, `installHook` now replaces a stale cledger block in\n286:   place instead of leaving any file containing the marker untouched — only the\n287:   marked region is rewritten, so surrounding user content survives.\n288: \n289:   *Not addressed here:* the read default. `cledger export` still defaults to\n290:   no reachability filter, and the fix belongs in `readEvents` rather than in\n291:   one CLI command, since library consumers (intent-recall) never go through\n292:   the CLI and would otherwise inherit the unscoped view. Separate entry below.\n293:   Per-branch remote refs (`…-branches/<branch>` plus a wildcard fetch refspec)\n294:   were considered and are *not* needed; they would only add fetch-side\n295:   selectivity, letting a collaborator decline a branch's conversations\n296:   entirely. Worth revisiting only if that is ever wanted.\n297: - **Reads default to unscoped** — `readEvents` applies reachability only when\n298:   the caller asks, and `cledger export` never asks, so an automated consumer\n299:   reading the ledger directly gets every branch's conversations unless it\n300:   knows to pass a rev. Now that push is scoped, this is the remaining half of\n301:   the same mismatch. The default belongs in `readEvents` (with an explicit\n302:   opt-out) rather than in `export`, precisely because the consumers that\n303:   matter — intent-recall and anything else using the library — never go\n304:   through the CLI at all.\n305: - **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n306:   repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n307:   fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n308:   like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n309:   a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n310:   secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n311:   it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n312:   cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n313:   tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n314:   no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n315:   recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n316:   not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n317:   should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n318:   allowlist for itself.\n319:   - **The false positive is self-perpetuating through documentation**\n320:     (observed 2026-07-27, from `intent-recall`): a design conversation that\n321:     quoted *this very roadmap entry* — the paragraph above, containing the\n322:     example `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")` — was itself flagged\n323:     with 6 `keyword-assignment` findings and held back from push. The\n324:     write-up of a false positive reproduces the false positive, in a\n325:     different repository, in a conversation transcript rather than in code.\n326:     Two things this exposes that the parent entry does not: (a) the allowlist\n327:     is per-repo, so a fingerprint retired here is re-encountered from scratch\n328:     by every downstream repo whose conversations read or discuss these docs —\n329:     and cledger's own documentation is now a reliable source of them; (b) the\n330:     flagged span was prose *about* a credential-shaped example and never a\n331:     credential, which is about as clean a signal as exists that the rule\n332:     wants a \"is this a discussion or an assignment?\" discriminator rather\n333:     than more allowlisting. Cheap partial fix worth weighing: treat a match\n334:     whose surrounding context is natural-language prose (a markdown\n335:     paragraph, a conversation turn) far more skeptically than one inside a\n336:     code block or a source file.\n337: - **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n338:   (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n339:   file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n340: - **`cledger redact`'s local purge leaves the value in the reflog** — the\n341:   squash is real but incomplete, and the command overstates what it achieved.\n342:   When the pre-redaction content was never pushed, `redactEvent` rebuilds the\n343:   notes ref as a parentless commit so the old chain becomes unreachable, and\n344:   reports `history squashed: yes`. Measured 2026-07-27: the pre-redaction blob\n345:   is still recoverable straight afterwards via\n346:   `git show $(git reflog show refs/notes/conversation-ledger | awk '{print $1}')`,\n347:   because `update-ref` leaves reflog entries pointing at the discarded\n348:   commits. It stays recoverable for the reflog expiry window (90 days by\n349:   default) and only actually disappears after\n350:   `git reflog expire --expire=now` plus a pruning `git gc`. So today's\n351:   redaction is \"no reader will surface it\" rather than \"it is gone\", even in\n352:   the case the tool claims full success for.\n353:   *Fix, needs a decision before implementing:* expiring the ledger ref's\n354:   reflog is narrow and safe, but reclaiming the objects needs a prune, and\n355:   prune is repo-wide — it would also drop unreachable objects the user may\n356:   have wanted (dropped stashes, abandoned commits). Options are a scoped\n357:   `reflog expire` plus `git prune` accepted as repo-wide, or leaving the\n358:   objects and telling the truth in the output instead. What must not stay as\n359:   it is: reporting `history squashed: yes` when the value is still one\n360:   `git show` away.\n361:   *Unchanged either way:* once the ref has been pushed the squash is skipped\n362:   entirely and rotating the credential is the only real remedy — see below.\n363: - **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n364:   shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n365:   pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n366:   far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n367:   holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n368:   must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n369:   detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n370:   unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n371:   force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n372:   the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n373:   `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n374:   purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n375:   happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n376: - **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n377:   history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n378:   `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n379:   so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n380:   transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n381:   the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n382:   suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n383:   candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n384:   corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n385:   abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n386: - **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n387:   preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n388:   with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n389:   directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n390:   scope and capture triggers.\n391: - **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n392:   detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n393:   dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n394:   re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n395:   current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n396:   appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n397:   it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n398:   live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n399:   live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n400:   append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n401:   auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n402:   (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n403: - **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n404:   broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n405:   through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n406:   naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n407:   Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n408:   did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n409:   matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n410:   characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n411:   (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n412:   teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n413:   be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n414:   is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n415:   promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n416:   rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n417:   makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n418:   intended narrow form.\n419: - **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n420:   confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n421:   not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n422:   plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n423:   disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n424:   the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n425:   Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n426:   capture runs silently on every turn.\n427: - **Share local state across worktrees** *(shipped, 0.13.0)* — cledger kept\n428:   its local state under `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`, which in a linked\n429:   worktree is that worktree's *private* directory\n430:   (`<main>/.git/worktrees/<name>/`), not the repo's shared one. Git is doing\n431:   exactly what it documents; the wrong assumption was that a repo has one git\n432:   dir. Everything cledger stores there is repo-wide, so a worktree behaved\n433:   like a separate repo, in four ways of descending severity: (a) the pre-push\n434:   hook was installed where **git never runs it** — git resolves hooks against\n435:   the common dir only — so a repo whose first capture happened in a worktree\n436:   got a hook that looked installed and silently never fired; (b) the\n437:   known-secrets store was invisible, failing **open**: values the user had\n438:   taught capture-time redaction to scrub were written to the ledger in the\n439:   clear, with nothing reporting it; (c) the allowlist was invisible, failing\n440:   closed, so already-dismissed scan findings blocked the ledger push again\n441:   (measured on this repo: 213 findings from a worktree vs 0 from the main\n442:   checkout); (d) capture cursors reset, re-scanning transcripts from the top.\n443:   Fixed by resolving a `commonDir` (`--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir`,\n444:   with a manual-resolve fallback for git < 2.31) and hanging all local state\n445:   and the hook path off it. Placement is irrelevant to the fix — git treats a\n446:   worktree nested under the repo and one at an arbitrary path identically, and\n447:   both are covered by tests. *Fallout that outlived the cause:* (d) also\n448:   **duplicated events**, because append-time dedup only consults the note of\n449:   the commit being written to, so a re-scan after HEAD moved filed a second\n450:   copy under a different anchor; once both anchors were reachable, reads\n451:   returned the turn twice (measured: 351 duplicated ids in this repo's own\n452:   ledger). The ledger is append-only, so those copies are permanent — reads\n453:   now collapse events by id, preferring the earliest `recorded_at` with the\n454:   canonical serialization as a deterministic tie-break. That path is not\n455:   worktree-specific; any forced rescan after HEAD moves can produce it.\n456:   *Deliberately unchanged:* `.cledger.json` stays a working-tree file, so it\n457:   is per-worktree unless committed — correct for versioned repo config, unlike\n458:   `.git`-side state. *Not migrated:* an allowlist or known-secrets store\n459:   written from a worktree before this fix stays orphaned in that worktree's\n460:   private dir; re-run `cledger allow` / `cledger redact --pattern` if you hit\n461:   it.\n462: - **Record which model/provider/CLI version served each turn** *(shipped,\n463:   0.12.0)* — `producer` previously said only which capture path recorded an\n464:   event (`source`, `session_id`, cledger's own `tool`/`version`), never what\n465:   produced it: the model, the inference provider, or the coding CLI's\n466:   version. Codex's `session_meta`/`turn_context` lines carry exactly that and\n467:   were dropped with no trace; Claude Code's per-line `version` and\n468:   `message.model` survived only inside `raw.data`, unqueryable without\n469:   parsing a source-native shape. Three optional fields close it —\n470:   `producer.model`, `producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — with\n471:   `cledger log --model M` to filter and the model(s) listed per row in\n472:   `cledger conversations`. *Only what the source states is recorded:*\n473:   `provider` stays unset for claude-code (never named in the transcript, and\n474:   the same CLI can point at the first-party API, Bedrock, or Vertex), a model\n475:   is never carried onto a turn the source did not label (so claude-code user\n476:   turns have none), and `\"<synthetic>\"` passes through verbatim rather than\n477:   being normalized away. Codex needed real work beyond reading a field: its\n478:   facts live on out-of-band lines, so a cursor-resumed capture re-scans the\n479:   file prefix to rebuild the context in force, and because `turn_context` is\n480:   written *after* the turn's opening messages in real rollouts, still-unset\n481:   fields are seeded from the earliest line stating them ahead — otherwise the\n482:   first user message of every session goes unlabelled. Seeding fills gaps\n483:   only; a genuine mid-session model switch still applies forward, never\n484:   retroactively. The fields are **excluded from event identity** (adding them\n485:   to the id would make a rescan duplicate every pre-upgrade turn instead of\n486:   deduping it), and scanning is unaffected since `scan`/capture-tier\n487:   redaction walk only `content` and `raw.data`. *Forward-only, and\n488:   deliberately no backfill:* rewriting `producer` on stored events would keep\n489:   their ids but change their note lines, and under `cat_sort_uniq` the\n490:   rewritten and original lines both survive a merge with any peer still\n491:   holding the old one — two copies of one event. Old claude-code events lose\n492:   nothing permanent (their whole source line is in `raw.data`); old codex\n493:   events do, and re-capturing them does not help, since they dedup by id.\n494:   *Out of scope:* the rest of what those codex lines carry — `base_instructions`\n495:   (the full system prompt), sandbox/approval policy, reasoning effort — is\n496:   still not captured. That is a separate question (whole-session\n497:   configuration, not per-turn agent identity) with its own size and\n498:   redaction implications; this cut answers only \"what served this turn\".\n499: - **Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state** *(shipped,\n500:   0.10.0)* — Codex's `reasoning` response_items carry `encrypted_content`\n501:   that only OpenAI's servers can decrypt; the client just round-trips the\n502:   blob, and replaying it restores the model's hidden reasoning\n503:   provider-side. Capture previously dropped these outright, which conflicted\n504:   with the record-everything-just-in-case posture: we discarded them because\n505:   *we* can't read them, yet the one party who can (the provider) already saw\n506:   the plaintext. Realization (2026-07-21, turnbridge): dropping them\n507:   permanently forecloses restoring reasoning when a Codex-origin\n508:   conversation is fabricated back into Codex on another machine (or after a\n509:   codex→claude→codex round trip). Empirically verified same-account and\n510:   cross-account (turnbridge `scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs`): a real\n511:   blob replayed in a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI\n512:   versions, and even a different paid ChatGPT account with identical token\n513:   accounting — the ciphertext is not keyed per-account. Shipped shape: a new\n514:   `reasoning` kind, provider-agnostic (not Codex-specific), raw-only —\n515:   `content` carries only an opacity marker, the blob lives solely in `raw`.\n516:   Rides the ledger's normal default-on sync like every other kind (no special\n517:   carve-out). `cledger export` includes it by default (lossless dump);\n518:   `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` hide it by default (`--with-reasoning`\n519:   to reveal) so ciphertext doesn't clutter human-facing transcripts. A\n520:   reasoning item's `summary` field (real, provider-decrypted plaintext when\n521:   reasoning-summary mode is on — distinct from `encrypted_content`) is split\n522:   out into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same seq, so it is\n523:   never hidden as if it were opaque. Capture-tier redaction and `cledger scan`\n524:   both exempt only the `encrypted_content` field from pattern-matching (it's\n525:   high-entropy by construction and this feature's entire point is preserving\n526:   it byte-exact — a coincidental rule match would silently corrupt it); every\n527:   other field, including `summary`, is scanned exactly like any other stored\n528:   content. Forward-only: sessions captured before 0.10.0 already had their\n529:   reasoning dropped and it's not recovered from what's on disk. *Remaining\n530:   open questions, mostly turnbridge's to answer at replay time:* blob TTL and\n531:   API-platform-org auth parity. *Deliberately out of scope here:* inter-agent\n532:   `agent_message` payloads still drop their embedded `encrypted_content`\n533:   blocks outright (same as before) rather than preserving them via this new\n534:   `reasoning` kind — same provider-withheld material, but embedded mid-line\n535:   rather than a standalone response_item, so preserving it needs its own\n536:   shape; tracked as a follow-up, not bundled into this cut. Also out of scope\n537:   at the time: no ledger event recorded which model/provider/CLI version\n538:   produced a given turn — a pre-existing gap in `Producer`, not specific to\n539:   reasoning, since closed in 0.12.0 (see the entry above). That gap mattered\n540:   most here: an encrypted blob is only replayable against the model that\n541:   produced it, so `reasoning` events captured before 0.12.0 carry ciphertext\n542:   with no record of where to send it.\n543: - **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n544: - **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n545:   discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n546:   commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n547:   with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n548:   the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n549:   remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n550:   exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n551:   lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n552:   extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n553:   piece.\n554: - **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n555:   that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n556:   event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n557:   conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n558:   \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n559:   reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n560:   forge credentials and per-host setup.\n561: \n562: ## Storage model, in one paragraph\n563: \n564: One git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n565: note body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n566: content inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n567: conflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n568: repository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n569: capture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n570: rewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n571: for details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n572: tool deliberately refuses to become.\n573: \n574: ## License\n575: \n576: [MIT](LICENSE)\n\n(End of file - total 576 lines)\n</content>","tool_use_id":"call_58307305508fb4b01059f162b72776dc","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"de79e501289e2e19946780867779ef465ead2124","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"opencode:ses_042f21ef9ffevc2ngWigi4VBsH","parent":"opencode:ses_042f492bbffe5WBNgLYhjw5VBZ","seq":13},"id":"ev1-618c19ec64df28ec958c1f2315bef4f54b71bc1595c8c7eecd36106fe1e37e18","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-01T11:23:17.803Z","producer":{"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","provider":"ds4","session_id":"ses_042f21ef9ffevc2ngWigi4VBsH","source":"opencode","source_version":"1.18.5","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"info":{"agent":"explore","cost":0,"finish":"tool-calls","id":"msg_fbd0ee3ac001Lv6wbIOHMyVsLB","mode":"explore","modelID":"deepseek-v4-flash","parentID":"msg_fbd0de10c001eMOaXzbSLnWvbQ","path":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","root":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger"},"providerID":"ds4","role":"assistant","sessionID":"ses_042f21ef9ffevc2ngWigi4VBsH","time":{"completed":1785583406405,"created":1785583297452},"tokens":{"cache":{"read":3528,"write":0},"input":3019,"output":315,"reasoning":0,"total":6862}},"part":{"callID":"call_58307305508fb4b01059f162b72776dc","id":"prt_fbd105ecf001wJfLO0u9Jyvb1u","messageID":"msg_fbd0ee3ac001Lv6wbIOHMyVsLB","sessionID":"ses_042f21ef9ffevc2ngWigi4VBsH","state":{"input":{"filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md"},"metadata":{"display":{"lineEnd":576,"lineStart":1,"path":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md","text":"# Conversation Ledger\n\nDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\nyour repository as git notes.\n\n`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\nutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\nconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\nconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\nsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\nride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\npre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n\nConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n\n- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n\nThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\nvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\ncd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\ncledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n```\n\n`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\nand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\nbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\nrepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\nidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\nduplicates.\n\nTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\ncapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\ninteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\ncodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n\nThe hooks above are installed once, globally, and fire in every git\nrepository you work in. To turn cledger off for one specific repo, add\n`{\"enabled\": false}` to that repo's `.cledger.json`: hook capture, manual\n`cledger append`, and backfill all become no-ops, before any ledger read or\nwrite happens. Existing history is untouched and still readable via\n`cledger log`/`show`/`export` — this only stops new events from being\nwritten. Outside any git repository, hooks are already a silent no-op, so\nthere's nothing to record and nothing to disable.\n\n## Use\n\n```sh\ncledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\ncledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\ncledger log --model gpt-5.6-sol # only turns a given model served\ncledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch, with the models they used\ncledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\ncledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\ncledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\ncledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\necho '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n```\n\n## Sharing (transport)\n\nThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n\n- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n- **Push is branch-scoped** (0.14.0): only conversations reachable from the\n  refs you're pushing are shared, so an abandoned or unmerged branch's\n  conversations stay on your machine. Squash-merged work still rides along —\n  the scope follows `re_anchor` mappings the same way reads do.\n  `cledger sync --all` pushes the whole ledger; `--rev R` scopes elsewhere.\n- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n  explicitly either way.\n\nIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\nare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\nthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\nand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n\n## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n\nEvents anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\nmerge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\nbranch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\nthe default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n\n- After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n  your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n  exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n  cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n  event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n  branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n  never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n  and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n- Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n  the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n  (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n  candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n  squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n  touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n  and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n  conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n  `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n  mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n  commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n  explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n  evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n  turns them off.\n- Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n  working either way.\n\n## Adapters\n\nSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n\nEvery captured event records the agent that served it — `producer.model`,\n`producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — but only where the source\nstates it; see the Roadmap entry below for what each adapter does and does\nnot know.\n\n| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Provider-encrypted `reasoning` items are preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events (hidden from `log`/`show` by default, see Roadmap); inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text but their embedded encrypted blocks are still dropped outright. |\n\nTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\nstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\nsystems):\n\n- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n\n## Security & redaction\n\nVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n\n### Redaction layers\n\n**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n\n**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n- Cost: manual configuration required.\n\n**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n\n**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n\n**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n\n### Commands\n\n`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n\n`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n\n### Maximum safety recipe\n\nKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n\n## Roadmap\n\n- **Config sections replace rather than merge, and it fails open** — `loadConfig`\n  merges `~/.config/cledger/config.json` and `<repo>/.cledger.json` per\n  *top-level section*, not per key (`const redact = override.redact ??\n  base.redact`). So a repo that commits a `.cledger.json` with *any* `redact`\n  key — a `patterns` list, say — silently replaces the user's entire `redact`\n  section, and a globally-enabled `redact.knownSecrets: true` reverts to its\n  default of `false`. Capture-time scrubbing of learned secret values then\n  stops in that repo, with nothing printed and nothing in the ledger to show\n  it happened. Same shape as the pre-0.13.0 worktree bug: silent, fails open,\n  visible only if you go looking. Fix is a deep merge within each section, or\n  at minimum a warning when a repo config overrides a section the user config\n  had already set. Worth doing together with a general sweep for this class.\n\n  **Self-referential failures are now this project's characteristic bug\n  family**, and are worth a standing check rather than four independent\n  fixes. cledger records the work of building cledger, so its own state,\n  output, and documentation feed back into its behavior. Known members: the\n  capture/scan redaction loop (a finding report re-seeding the finding it\n  describes — closed in 0.6.1/0.7.0); the pre-0.13.0 worktree state split\n  (0.13.0); this config section-replace; and the allowlist entry below, whose\n  *write-up* of a false positive reproduces the false positive in every repo\n  whose conversations quote it. Three of the four fail silently, and two fail\n  open. A useful tripwire when adding anything that reads cledger's own\n  state or prints cledger's own findings: ask what happens when that output\n  is itself captured.\n- **Don't print findings by default; print a pointer to them** — the cleanest\n  structural break in the self-referential loop above. Today `cledger scan`\n  and the sync gate print every finding inline, which is exactly the text that\n  gets captured into the next conversation and re-seeds the finding. Masking\n  the matched span (0.6.1) removed the secret characters but not the\n  *surrounding* credential-shaped context, which is what\n  `keyword-assignment` trips on — so the loop is narrowed, not closed.\n  Proposed: report only the count and how to inspect, with the audience split\n  made explicit in the text — a human is told which command reveals the\n  findings; an agent is told not to run it, because reading the findings into\n  its context is what reproduces them, and to surface the count to its human\n  instead. Naming the failure mode in the output is the point: an agent that\n  understands *why* will comply, where a bare \"don't look\" invites a\n  workaround. Pairs naturally with the inspection command in the design doc's\n  open questions, which already has to write unmasked output to a file rather\n  than stdout for the same reason. *Tension to resolve first:* CI reads those\n  printed findings, and `cledger scan`'s nonzero exit plus inline report is\n  the documented CI integration — so the default likely has to be\n  terminal-aware, or CI has to opt in explicitly (`--report`), rather than\n  simply going quiet for everyone.\n- **Branch-scoped push** *(shipped, 0.14.0)* — reachability filtered *reads*\n  (`cledger log`, `show`, `conversations`), but the notes ref is a single ref\n  and a ref push sends all of it, so conversations from a branch that was\n  abandoned and never merged still reached `origin` and landed on every\n  collaborator's disk. Two scopes wearing one name, with push the odd one out:\n  storage was already per-commit and reads already per-branch.\n\n  Push now carries only the anchors a read at that rev resolves to. Storage is\n  unchanged (commit-anchored, one local ref, one shared remote ref) — only\n  push learns the granularity storage already had. `cledger sync` scopes to\n  the current branch by default, `--rev` scopes elsewhere, `--all` restores\n  the whole-ledger push. **No mapping is stored anywhere:** reachability is\n  recomputed at push time from the DAG, the same reason reads never stored it.\n\n  *Squash merges are the case that decides the implementation.* The scope\n  reuses `resolveAnchors` — the same function reads use — rather than a plain\n  `git rev-list`. A squash discards the source branch's commits, so its\n  conversations sit on anchors unreachable from the target, and only the\n  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the *surviving* commit) tie them\n  back. A rev-list-only scope would push those mapping events while leaving\n  behind the events they point at — dropping exactly what re-anchoring exists\n  to save. Covered by a test that fails against a rev-list-only scope.\n\n  *The destructive shortcut, recorded because it is the natural first\n  implementation.* A notes ref is a snapshot of the whole database, not an\n  append log: its tip tree maps every annotated commit to its note, so\n  whatever tree you push becomes the remote's entire notes database. Filtering\n  your local ref down to one branch and pushing that still fast-forwards — and\n  silently deletes every other branch's conversations from the remote's\n  current view (they survive in the ref's history but vanish from `git notes\n  show` and from any fresh clone). The scoped push therefore seeds a temp ref\n  from the remote's tip and unions its scope *into* that, so the push strictly\n  adds. Also covered by a test that fails against the filtered-tree version.\n\n  *Side benefit:* the pre-push scan gate now inspects only what the push would\n  carry, so a finding in a branch that is not being pushed no longer blocks\n  shipping unrelated work.\n\n  *Scope follows the refs actually being pushed* (0.15.0). The hook is fed\n  git's pre-push stdin — one line per ref — so `git push origin feat` from\n  another branch shares feat's conversations. 0.14.0 shipped with `</dev/null`\n  and scoped by `HEAD`, which shared nothing in that case. Ref deletions are\n  skipped (no tip to scope to), multiple refs union, and anything unusable\n  falls back to `HEAD` rather than to the whole ledger. Because this changes\n  the installed hook, `installHook` now replaces a stale cledger block in\n  place instead of leaving any file containing the marker untouched — only the\n  marked region is rewritten, so surrounding user content survives.\n\n  *Not addressed here:* the read default. `cledger export` still defaults to\n  no reachability filter, and the fix belongs in `readEvents` rather than in\n  one CLI command, since library consumers (intent-recall) never go through\n  the CLI and would otherwise inherit the unscoped view. Separate entry below.\n  Per-branch remote refs (`…-branches/<branch>` plus a wildcard fetch refspec)\n  were considered and are *not* needed; they would only add fetch-side\n  selectivity, letting a collaborator decline a branch's conversations\n  entirely. Worth revisiting only if that is ever wanted.\n- **Reads default to unscoped** — `readEvents` applies reachability only when\n  the caller asks, and `cledger export` never asks, so an automated consumer\n  reading the ledger directly gets every branch's conversations unless it\n  knows to pass a rev. Now that push is scoped, this is the remaining half of\n  the same mismatch. The default belongs in `readEvents` (with an explicit\n  opt-out) rather than in `export`, precisely because the consumers that\n  matter — intent-recall and anything else using the library — never go\n  through the CLI at all.\n- **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n  repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n  fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n  like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n  a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n  secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n  it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n  cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n  tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n  no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n  recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n  not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n  should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n  allowlist for itself.\n  - **The false positive is self-perpetuating through documentation**\n    (observed 2026-07-27, from `intent-recall`): a design conversation that\n    quoted *this very roadmap entry* — the paragraph above, containing the\n    example `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")` — was itself flagged\n    with 6 `keyword-assignment` findings and held back from push. The\n    write-up of a false positive reproduces the false positive, in a\n    different repository, in a conversation transcript rather than in code.\n    Two things this exposes that the parent entry does not: (a) the allowlist\n    is per-repo, so a fingerprint retired here is re-encountered from scratch\n    by every downstream repo whose conversations read or discuss these docs —\n    and cledger's own documentation is now a reliable source of them; (b) the\n    flagged span was prose *about* a credential-shaped example and never a\n    credential, which is about as clean a signal as exists that the rule\n    wants a \"is this a discussion or an assignment?\" discriminator rather\n    than more allowlisting. Cheap partial fix worth weighing: treat a match\n    whose surrounding context is natural-language prose (a markdown\n    paragraph, a conversation turn) far more skeptically than one inside a\n    code block or a source file.\n- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n- **`cledger redact`'s local purge leaves the value in the reflog** — the\n  squash is real but incomplete, and the command overstates what it achieved.\n  When the pre-redaction content was never pushed, `redactEvent` rebuilds the\n  notes ref as a parentless commit so the old chain becomes unreachable, and\n  reports `history squashed: yes`. Measured 2026-07-27: the pre-redaction blob\n  is still recoverable straight afterwards via\n  `git show $(git reflog show refs/notes/conversation-ledger | awk '{print $1}')`,\n  because `update-ref` leaves reflog entries pointing at the discarded\n  commits. It stays recoverable for the reflog expiry window (90 days by\n  default) and only actually disappears after\n  `git reflog expire --expire=now` plus a pruning `git gc`. So today's\n  redaction is \"no reader will surface it\" rather than \"it is gone\", even in\n  the case the tool claims full success for.\n  *Fix, needs a decision before implementing:* expiring the ledger ref's\n  reflog is narrow and safe, but reclaiming the objects needs a prune, and\n  prune is repo-wide — it would also drop unreachable objects the user may\n  have wanted (dropped stashes, abandoned commits). Options are a scoped\n  `reflog expire` plus `git prune` accepted as repo-wide, or leaving the\n  objects and telling the truth in the output instead. What must not stay as\n  it is: reporting `history squashed: yes` when the value is still one\n  `git show` away.\n  *Unchanged either way:* once the ref has been pushed the squash is skipped\n  entirely and rotating the credential is the only real remedy — see below.\n- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n  far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n  holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n  must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n  detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n  unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n  force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n  the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n  `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n  purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n  happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n- **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n  history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n  so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n  transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n  the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n  suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n  candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n  corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n  abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n  scope and capture triggers.\n- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n  intended narrow form.\n- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n  capture runs silently on every turn.\n- **Share local state across worktrees** *(shipped, 0.13.0)* — cledger kept\n  its local state under `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`, which in a linked\n  worktree is that worktree's *private* directory\n  (`<main>/.git/worktrees/<name>/`), not the repo's shared one. Git is doing\n  exactly what it documents; the wrong assumption was that a repo has one git\n  dir. Everything cledger stores there is repo-wide, so a worktree behaved\n  like a separate repo, in four ways of descending severity: (a) the pre-push\n  hook was installed where **git never runs it** — git resolves hooks against\n  the common dir only — so a repo whose first capture happened in a worktree\n  got a hook that looked installed and silently never fired; (b) the\n  known-secrets store was invisible, failing **open**: values the user had\n  taught capture-time redaction to scrub were written to the ledger in the\n  clear, with nothing reporting it; (c) the allowlist was invisible, failing\n  closed, so already-dismissed scan findings blocked the ledger push again\n  (measured on this repo: 213 findings from a worktree vs 0 from the main\n  checkout); (d) capture cursors reset, re-scanning transcripts from the top.\n  Fixed by resolving a `commonDir` (`--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir`,\n  with a manual-resolve fallback for git < 2.31) and hanging all local state\n  and the hook path off it. Placement is irrelevant to the fix — git treats a\n  worktree nested under the repo and one at an arbitrary path identically, and\n  both are covered by tests. *Fallout that outlived the cause:* (d) also\n  **duplicated events**, because append-time dedup only consults the note of\n  the commit being written to, so a re-scan after HEAD moved filed a second\n  copy under a different anchor; once both anchors were reachable, reads\n  returned the turn twice (measured: 351 duplicated ids in this repo's own\n  ledger). The ledger is append-only, so those copies are permanent — reads\n  now collapse events by id, preferring the earliest `recorded_at` with the\n  canonical serialization as a deterministic tie-break. That path is not\n  worktree-specific; any forced rescan after HEAD moves can produce it.\n  *Deliberately unchanged:* `.cledger.json` stays a working-tree file, so it\n  is per-worktree unless committed — correct for versioned repo config, unlike\n  `.git`-side state. *Not migrated:* an allowlist or known-secrets store\n  written from a worktree before this fix stays orphaned in that worktree's\n  private dir; re-run `cledger allow` / `cledger redact --pattern` if you hit\n  it.\n- **Record which model/provider/CLI version served each turn** *(shipped,\n  0.12.0)* — `producer` previously said only which capture path recorded an\n  event (`source`, `session_id`, cledger's own `tool`/`version`), never what\n  produced it: the model, the inference provider, or the coding CLI's\n  version. Codex's `session_meta`/`turn_context` lines carry exactly that and\n  were dropped with no trace; Claude Code's per-line `version` and\n  `message.model` survived only inside `raw.data`, unqueryable without\n  parsing a source-native shape. Three optional fields close it —\n  `producer.model`, `producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — with\n  `cledger log --model M` to filter and the model(s) listed per row in\n  `cledger conversations`. *Only what the source states is recorded:*\n  `provider` stays unset for claude-code (never named in the transcript, and\n  the same CLI can point at the first-party API, Bedrock, or Vertex), a model\n  is never carried onto a turn the source did not label (so claude-code user\n  turns have none), and `\"<synthetic>\"` passes through verbatim rather than\n  being normalized away. Codex needed real work beyond reading a field: its\n  facts live on out-of-band lines, so a cursor-resumed capture re-scans the\n  file prefix to rebuild the context in force, and because `turn_context` is\n  written *after* the turn's opening messages in real rollouts, still-unset\n  fields are seeded from the earliest line stating them ahead — otherwise the\n  first user message of every session goes unlabelled. Seeding fills gaps\n  only; a genuine mid-session model switch still applies forward, never\n  retroactively. The fields are **excluded from event identity** (adding them\n  to the id would make a rescan duplicate every pre-upgrade turn instead of\n  deduping it), and scanning is unaffected since `scan`/capture-tier\n  redaction walk only `content` and `raw.data`. *Forward-only, and\n  deliberately no backfill:* rewriting `producer` on stored events would keep\n  their ids but change their note lines, and under `cat_sort_uniq` the\n  rewritten and original lines both survive a merge with any peer still\n  holding the old one — two copies of one event. Old claude-code events lose\n  nothing permanent (their whole source line is in `raw.data`); old codex\n  events do, and re-capturing them does not help, since they dedup by id.\n  *Out of scope:* the rest of what those codex lines carry — `base_instructions`\n  (the full system prompt), sandbox/approval policy, reasoning effort — is\n  still not captured. That is a separate question (whole-session\n  configuration, not per-turn agent identity) with its own size and\n  redaction implications; this cut answers only \"what served this turn\".\n- **Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state** *(shipped,\n  0.10.0)* — Codex's `reasoning` response_items carry `encrypted_content`\n  that only OpenAI's servers can decrypt; the client just round-trips the\n  blob, and replaying it restores the model's hidden reasoning\n  provider-side. Capture previously dropped these outright, which conflicted\n  with the record-everything-just-in-case posture: we discarded them because\n  *we* can't read them, yet the one party who can (the provider) already saw\n  the plaintext. Realization (2026-07-21, turnbridge): dropping them\n  permanently forecloses restoring reasoning when a Codex-origin\n  conversation is fabricated back into Codex on another machine (or after a\n  codex→claude→codex round trip). Empirically verified same-account and\n  cross-account (turnbridge `scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs`): a real\n  blob replayed in a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI\n  versions, and even a different paid ChatGPT account with identical token\n  accounting — the ciphertext is not keyed per-account. Shipped shape: a new\n  `reasoning` kind, provider-agnostic (not Codex-specific), raw-only —\n  `content` carries only an opacity marker, the blob lives solely in `raw`.\n  Rides the ledger's normal default-on sync like every other kind (no special\n  carve-out). `cledger export` includes it by default (lossless dump);\n  `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` hide it by default (`--with-reasoning`\n  to reveal) so ciphertext doesn't clutter human-facing transcripts. A\n  reasoning item's `summary` field (real, provider-decrypted plaintext when\n  reasoning-summary mode is on — distinct from `encrypted_content`) is split\n  out into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same seq, so it is\n  never hidden as if it were opaque. Capture-tier redaction and `cledger scan`\n  both exempt only the `encrypted_content` field from pattern-matching (it's\n  high-entropy by construction and this feature's entire point is preserving\n  it byte-exact — a coincidental rule match would silently corrupt it); every\n  other field, including `summary`, is scanned exactly like any other stored\n  content. Forward-only: sessions captured before 0.10.0 already had their\n  reasoning dropped and it's not recovered from what's on disk. *Remaining\n  open questions, mostly turnbridge's to answer at replay time:* blob TTL and\n  API-platform-org auth parity. *Deliberately out of scope here:* inter-agent\n  `agent_message` payloads still drop their embedded `encrypted_content`\n  blocks outright (same as before) rather than preserving them via this new\n  `reasoning` kind — same provider-withheld material, but embedded mid-line\n  rather than a standalone response_item, so preserving it needs its own\n  shape; tracked as a follow-up, not bundled into this cut. Also out of scope\n  at the time: no ledger event recorded which model/provider/CLI version\n  produced a given turn — a pre-existing gap in `Producer`, not specific to\n  reasoning, since closed in 0.12.0 (see the entry above). That gap mattered\n  most here: an encrypted blob is only replayable against the model that\n  produced it, so `reasoning` events captured before 0.12.0 carry ciphertext\n  with no record of where to send it.\n- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n- **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n  discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n  commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n  with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n  the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n  remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n  exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n  lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n  extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n  piece.\n- **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n  that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n  event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n  conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n  \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n  reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n  forge credentials and per-host setup.\n\n## Storage model, in one paragraph\n\nOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\nnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\ncontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\nconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\nrepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\ncapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\nrewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\nfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\ntool deliberately refuses to become.\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE)","totalLines":576,"truncated":false,"type":"file"},"loaded":[],"preview":"# Conversation Ledger\n\nDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\nyour repository as git notes.\n\n`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\nutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\nconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\nconversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\nsquash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\nride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\npre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n\nConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n\n- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n","truncated":false},"output":"<path>/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Conversation Ledger\n2: \n3: Durable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4: your repository as git notes.\n5: \n6: `cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7: utility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8: `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9: conversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10: conversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11: squash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12: ride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13: pre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14: \n15: Conversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16: \n17: - `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18: - `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19: - `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20: \n21: The ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22: visibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23: \n24: ## Install\n25: \n26: ```sh\n27: git clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28: cd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29: cledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n30: ```\n31: \n32: `cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n33: and a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n34: backing both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n35: repository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n36: idempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n37: duplicates.\n38: \n39: Two one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n40: capture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n41: interactively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n42: codex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n43: <source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n44: \n45: The hooks above are installed once, globally, and fire in every git\n46: repository you work in. To turn cledger off for one specific repo, add\n47: `{\"enabled\": false}` to that repo's `.cledger.json`: hook capture, manual\n48: `cledger append`, and backfill all become no-ops, before any ledger read or\n49: write happens. Existing history is untouched and still readable via\n50: `cledger log`/`show`/`export` — this only stops new events from being\n51: written. Outside any git repository, hooks are already a silent no-op, so\n52: there's nothing to record and nothing to disable.\n53: \n54: ## Use\n55: \n56: ```sh\n57: cledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n58: cledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n59: cledger log --model gpt-5.6-sol # only turns a given model served\n60: cledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch, with the models they used\n61: cledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n62: cledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n63: cledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n64: cledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n65: echo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n66: ```\n67: \n68: ## Sharing (transport)\n69: \n70: The ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n71: \n72: - **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n73:   safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n74:   `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n75:   ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n76:   ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n77:   `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n78: - **Push is branch-scoped** (0.14.0): only conversations reachable from the\n79:   refs you're pushing are shared, so an abandoned or unmerged branch's\n80:   conversations stay on your machine. Squash-merged work still rides along —\n81:   the scope follows `re_anchor` mappings the same way reads do.\n82:   `cledger sync --all` pushes the whole ledger; `--rev R` scopes elsewhere.\n83: - **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n84:   `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n85:   (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n86:   `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n87: - **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n88:   `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n89:   marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n90:   explicitly either way.\n91: \n92: If git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n93: are recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n94: the same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n95: and never guesses a hostname-based one.\n96: \n97: ## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n98: \n99: Events anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n100: merge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n101: branch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n102: the default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n103: \n104: - After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n105:   your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n106:   exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n107:   cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n108:   event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n109:   branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n110:   never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n111:   and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n112: - Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n113:   the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n114:   (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n115:   candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n116:   squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n117:   touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n118:   and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n119:   conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n120:   `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n121:   mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n122:   commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n123:   explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n124:   evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n125:   turns them off.\n126: - Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n127:   working either way.\n128: \n129: ## Adapters\n130: \n131: Supported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n132: \n133: Every captured event records the agent that served it — `producer.model`,\n134: `producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — but only where the source\n135: states it; see the Roadmap entry below for what each adapter does and does\n136: not know.\n137: \n138: | Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n139: |---|---|---|---|\n140: | Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n141: | Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Provider-encrypted `reasoning` items are preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events (hidden from `log`/`show` by default, see Roadmap); inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text but their embedded encrypted blocks are still dropped outright. |\n142: \n143: TODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n144: story looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n145: systems):\n146: \n147: - **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n148: - **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n149: - **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n150: - **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n151: - **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n152: - **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n153: - **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n154: - **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n155: - **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n156: \n157: ## Security & redaction\n158: \n159: Visible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n160: \n161: ### Redaction layers\n162: \n163: **Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n164: - Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n165: - Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n166: \n167: **Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n168: - Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n169: - Cost: manual configuration required.\n170: \n171: **Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n172: - Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n173: - Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n174: \n175: **Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n176: - Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n177: - Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n178: \n179: **Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n180: - Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n181: - Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n182: - Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n183: \n184: ### Commands\n185: \n186: `cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n187: \n188: `cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n189: \n190: ### Maximum safety recipe\n191: \n192: Keep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n193: \n194: ## Roadmap\n195: \n196: - **Config sections replace rather than merge, and it fails open** — `loadConfig`\n197:   merges `~/.config/cledger/config.json` and `<repo>/.cledger.json` per\n198:   *top-level section*, not per key (`const redact = override.redact ??\n199:   base.redact`). So a repo that commits a `.cledger.json` with *any* `redact`\n200:   key — a `patterns` list, say — silently replaces the user's entire `redact`\n201:   section, and a globally-enabled `redact.knownSecrets: true` reverts to its\n202:   default of `false`. Capture-time scrubbing of learned secret values then\n203:   stops in that repo, with nothing printed and nothing in the ledger to show\n204:   it happened. Same shape as the pre-0.13.0 worktree bug: silent, fails open,\n205:   visible only if you go looking. Fix is a deep merge within each section, or\n206:   at minimum a warning when a repo config overrides a section the user config\n207:   had already set. Worth doing together with a general sweep for this class.\n208: \n209:   **Self-referential failures are now this project's characteristic bug\n210:   family**, and are worth a standing check rather than four independent\n211:   fixes. cledger records the work of building cledger, so its own state,\n212:   output, and documentation feed back into its behavior. Known members: the\n213:   capture/scan redaction loop (a finding report re-seeding the finding it\n214:   describes — closed in 0.6.1/0.7.0); the pre-0.13.0 worktree state split\n215:   (0.13.0); this config section-replace; and the allowlist entry below, whose\n216:   *write-up* of a false positive reproduces the false positive in every repo\n217:   whose conversations quote it. Three of the four fail silently, and two fail\n218:   open. A useful tripwire when adding anything that reads cledger's own\n219:   state or prints cledger's own findings: ask what happens when that output\n220:   is itself captured.\n221: - **Don't print findings by default; print a pointer to them** — the cleanest\n222:   structural break in the self-referential loop above. Today `cledger scan`\n223:   and the sync gate print every finding inline, which is exactly the text that\n224:   gets captured into the next conversation and re-seeds the finding. Masking\n225:   the matched span (0.6.1) removed the secret characters but not the\n226:   *surrounding* credential-shaped context, which is what\n227:   `keyword-assignment` trips on — so the loop is narrowed, not closed.\n228:   Proposed: report only the count and how to inspect, with the audience split\n229:   made explicit in the text — a human is told which command reveals the\n230:   findings; an agent is told not to run it, because reading the findings into\n231:   its context is what reproduces them, and to surface the count to its human\n232:   instead. Naming the failure mode in the output is the point: an agent that\n233:   understands *why* will comply, where a bare \"don't look\" invites a\n234:   workaround. Pairs naturally with the inspection command in the design doc's\n235:   open questions, which already has to write unmasked output to a file rather\n236:   than stdout for the same reason. *Tension to resolve first:* CI reads those\n237:   printed findings, and `cledger scan`'s nonzero exit plus inline report is\n238:   the documented CI integration — so the default likely has to be\n239:   terminal-aware, or CI has to opt in explicitly (`--report`), rather than\n240:   simply going quiet for everyone.\n241: - **Branch-scoped push** *(shipped, 0.14.0)* — reachability filtered *reads*\n242:   (`cledger log`, `show`, `conversations`), but the notes ref is a single ref\n243:   and a ref push sends all of it, so conversations from a branch that was\n244:   abandoned and never merged still reached `origin` and landed on every\n245:   collaborator's disk. Two scopes wearing one name, with push the odd one out:\n246:   storage was already per-commit and reads already per-branch.\n247: \n248:   Push now carries only the anchors a read at that rev resolves to. Storage is\n249:   unchanged (commit-anchored, one local ref, one shared remote ref) — only\n250:   push learns the granularity storage already had. `cledger sync` scopes to\n251:   the current branch by default, `--rev` scopes elsewhere, `--all` restores\n252:   the whole-ledger push. **No mapping is stored anywhere:** reachability is\n253:   recomputed at push time from the DAG, the same reason reads never stored it.\n254: \n255:   *Squash merges are the case that decides the implementation.* The scope\n256:   reuses `resolveAnchors` — the same function reads use — rather than a plain\n257:   `git rev-list`. A squash discards the source branch's commits, so its\n258:   conversations sit on anchors unreachable from the target, and only the\n259:   `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the *surviving* commit) tie them\n260:   back. A rev-list-only scope would push those mapping events while leaving\n261:   behind the events they point at — dropping exactly what re-anchoring exists\n262:   to save. Covered by a test that fails against a rev-list-only scope.\n263: \n264:   *The destructive shortcut, recorded because it is the natural first\n265:   implementation.* A notes ref is a snapshot of the whole database, not an\n266:   append log: its tip tree maps every annotated commit to its note, so\n267:   whatever tree you push becomes the remote's entire notes database. Filtering\n268:   your local ref down to one branch and pushing that still fast-forwards — and\n269:   silently deletes every other branch's conversations from the remote's\n270:   current view (they survive in the ref's history but vanish from `git notes\n271:   show` and from any fresh clone). The scoped push therefore seeds a temp ref\n272:   from the remote's tip and unions its scope *into* that, so the push strictly\n273:   adds. Also covered by a test that fails against the filtered-tree version.\n274: \n275:   *Side benefit:* the pre-push scan gate now inspects only what the push would\n276:   carry, so a finding in a branch that is not being pushed no longer blocks\n277:   shipping unrelated work.\n278: \n279:   *Scope follows the refs actually being pushed* (0.15.0). The hook is fed\n280:   git's pre-push stdin — one line per ref — so `git push origin feat` from\n281:   another branch shares feat's conversations. 0.14.0 shipped with `</dev/null`\n282:   and scoped by `HEAD`, which shared nothing in that case. Ref deletions are\n283:   skipped (no tip to scope to), multiple refs union, and anything unusable\n284:   falls back to `HEAD` rather than to the whole ledger. Because this changes\n285:   the installed hook, `installHook` now replaces a stale cledger block in\n286:   place instead of leaving any file containing the marker untouched — only the\n287:   marked region is rewritten, so surrounding user content survives.\n288: \n289:   *Not addressed here:* the read default. `cledger export` still defaults to\n290:   no reachability filter, and the fix belongs in `readEvents` rather than in\n291:   one CLI command, since library consumers (intent-recall) never go through\n292:   the CLI and would otherwise inherit the unscoped view. Separate entry below.\n293:   Per-branch remote refs (`…-branches/<branch>` plus a wildcard fetch refspec)\n294:   were considered and are *not* needed; they would only add fetch-side\n295:   selectivity, letting a collaborator decline a branch's conversations\n296:   entirely. Worth revisiting only if that is ever wanted.\n297: - **Reads default to unscoped** — `readEvents` applies reachability only when\n298:   the caller asks, and `cledger export` never asks, so an automated consumer\n299:   reading the ledger directly gets every branch's conversations unless it\n300:   knows to pass a rev. Now that push is scoped, this is the remaining half of\n301:   the same mismatch. The default belongs in `readEvents` (with an explicit\n302:   opt-out) rather than in `export`, precisely because the consumers that\n303:   matter — intent-recall and anything else using the library — never go\n304:   through the CLI at all.\n305: - **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n306:   repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n307:   fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n308:   like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n309:   a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n310:   secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n311:   it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n312:   cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n313:   tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n314:   no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n315:   recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n316:   not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n317:   should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n318:   allowlist for itself.\n319:   - **The false positive is self-perpetuating through documentation**\n320:     (observed 2026-07-27, from `intent-recall`): a design conversation that\n321:     quoted *this very roadmap entry* — the paragraph above, containing the\n322:     example `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")` — was itself flagged\n323:     with 6 `keyword-assignment` findings and held back from push. The\n324:     write-up of a false positive reproduces the false positive, in a\n325:     different repository, in a conversation transcript rather than in code.\n326:     Two things this exposes that the parent entry does not: (a) the allowlist\n327:     is per-repo, so a fingerprint retired here is re-encountered from scratch\n328:     by every downstream repo whose conversations read or discuss these docs —\n329:     and cledger's own documentation is now a reliable source of them; (b) the\n330:     flagged span was prose *about* a credential-shaped example and never a\n331:     credential, which is about as clean a signal as exists that the rule\n332:     wants a \"is this a discussion or an assignment?\" discriminator rather\n333:     than more allowlisting. Cheap partial fix worth weighing: treat a match\n334:     whose surrounding context is natural-language prose (a markdown\n335:     paragraph, a conversation turn) far more skeptically than one inside a\n336:     code block or a source file.\n337: - **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n338:   (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n339:   file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n340: - **`cledger redact`'s local purge leaves the value in the reflog** — the\n341:   squash is real but incomplete, and the command overstates what it achieved.\n342:   When the pre-redaction content was never pushed, `redactEvent` rebuilds the\n343:   notes ref as a parentless commit so the old chain becomes unreachable, and\n344:   reports `history squashed: yes`. Measured 2026-07-27: the pre-redaction blob\n345:   is still recoverable straight afterwards via\n346:   `git show $(git reflog show refs/notes/conversation-ledger | awk '{print $1}')`,\n347:   because `update-ref` leaves reflog entries pointing at the discarded\n348:   commits. It stays recoverable for the reflog expiry window (90 days by\n349:   default) and only actually disappears after\n350:   `git reflog expire --expire=now` plus a pruning `git gc`. So today's\n351:   redaction is \"no reader will surface it\" rather than \"it is gone\", even in\n352:   the case the tool claims full success for.\n353:   *Fix, needs a decision before implementing:* expiring the ledger ref's\n354:   reflog is narrow and safe, but reclaiming the objects needs a prune, and\n355:   prune is repo-wide — it would also drop unreachable objects the user may\n356:   have wanted (dropped stashes, abandoned commits). Options are a scoped\n357:   `reflog expire` plus `git prune` accepted as repo-wide, or leaving the\n358:   objects and telling the truth in the output instead. What must not stay as\n359:   it is: reporting `history squashed: yes` when the value is still one\n360:   `git show` away.\n361:   *Unchanged either way:* once the ref has been pushed the squash is skipped\n362:   entirely and rotating the credential is the only real remedy — see below.\n363: - **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n364:   shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n365:   pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n366:   far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n367:   holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n368:   must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n369:   detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n370:   unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n371:   force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n372:   the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n373:   `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n374:   purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n375:   happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n376: - **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n377:   history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n378:   `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n379:   so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n380:   transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n381:   the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n382:   suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n383:   candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n384:   corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n385:   abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n386: - **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n387:   preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n388:   with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n389:   directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n390:   scope and capture triggers.\n391: - **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n392:   detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n393:   dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n394:   re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n395:   current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n396:   appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n397:   it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n398:   live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n399:   live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n400:   append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n401:   auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n402:   (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n403: - **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n404:   broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n405:   through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n406:   naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n407:   Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n408:   did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n409:   matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n410:   characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n411:   (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n412:   teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n413:   be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n414:   is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n415:   promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n416:   rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n417:   makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n418:   intended narrow form.\n419: - **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n420:   confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n421:   not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n422:   plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n423:   disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n424:   the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n425:   Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n426:   capture runs silently on every turn.\n427: - **Share local state across worktrees** *(shipped, 0.13.0)* — cledger kept\n428:   its local state under `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`, which in a linked\n429:   worktree is that worktree's *private* directory\n430:   (`<main>/.git/worktrees/<name>/`), not the repo's shared one. Git is doing\n431:   exactly what it documents; the wrong assumption was that a repo has one git\n432:   dir. Everything cledger stores there is repo-wide, so a worktree behaved\n433:   like a separate repo, in four ways of descending severity: (a) the pre-push\n434:   hook was installed where **git never runs it** — git resolves hooks against\n435:   the common dir only — so a repo whose first capture happened in a worktree\n436:   got a hook that looked installed and silently never fired; (b) the\n437:   known-secrets store was invisible, failing **open**: values the user had\n438:   taught capture-time redaction to scrub were written to the ledger in the\n439:   clear, with nothing reporting it; (c) the allowlist was invisible, failing\n440:   closed, so already-dismissed scan findings blocked the ledger push again\n441:   (measured on this repo: 213 findings from a worktree vs 0 from the main\n442:   checkout); (d) capture cursors reset, re-scanning transcripts from the top.\n443:   Fixed by resolving a `commonDir` (`--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir`,\n444:   with a manual-resolve fallback for git < 2.31) and hanging all local state\n445:   and the hook path off it. Placement is irrelevant to the fix — git treats a\n446:   worktree nested under the repo and one at an arbitrary path identically, and\n447:   both are covered by tests. *Fallout that outlived the cause:* (d) also\n448:   **duplicated events**, because append-time dedup only consults the note of\n449:   the commit being written to, so a re-scan after HEAD moved filed a second\n450:   copy under a different anchor; once both anchors were reachable, reads\n451:   returned the turn twice (measured: 351 duplicated ids in this repo's own\n452:   ledger). The ledger is append-only, so those copies are permanent — reads\n453:   now collapse events by id, preferring the earliest `recorded_at` with the\n454:   canonical serialization as a deterministic tie-break. That path is not\n455:   worktree-specific; any forced rescan after HEAD moves can produce it.\n456:   *Deliberately unchanged:* `.cledger.json` stays a working-tree file, so it\n457:   is per-worktree unless committed — correct for versioned repo config, unlike\n458:   `.git`-side state. *Not migrated:* an allowlist or known-secrets store\n459:   written from a worktree before this fix stays orphaned in that worktree's\n460:   private dir; re-run `cledger allow` / `cledger redact --pattern` if you hit\n461:   it.\n462: - **Record which model/provider/CLI version served each turn** *(shipped,\n463:   0.12.0)* — `producer` previously said only which capture path recorded an\n464:   event (`source`, `session_id`, cledger's own `tool`/`version`), never what\n465:   produced it: the model, the inference provider, or the coding CLI's\n466:   version. Codex's `session_meta`/`turn_context` lines carry exactly that and\n467:   were dropped with no trace; Claude Code's per-line `version` and\n468:   `message.model` survived only inside `raw.data`, unqueryable without\n469:   parsing a source-native shape. Three optional fields close it —\n470:   `producer.model`, `producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — with\n471:   `cledger log --model M` to filter and the model(s) listed per row in\n472:   `cledger conversations`. *Only what the source states is recorded:*\n473:   `provider` stays unset for claude-code (never named in the transcript, and\n474:   the same CLI can point at the first-party API, Bedrock, or Vertex), a model\n475:   is never carried onto a turn the source did not label (so claude-code user\n476:   turns have none), and `\"<synthetic>\"` passes through verbatim rather than\n477:   being normalized away. Codex needed real work beyond reading a field: its\n478:   facts live on out-of-band lines, so a cursor-resumed capture re-scans the\n479:   file prefix to rebuild the context in force, and because `turn_context` is\n480:   written *after* the turn's opening messages in real rollouts, still-unset\n481:   fields are seeded from the earliest line stating them ahead — otherwise the\n482:   first user message of every session goes unlabelled. Seeding fills gaps\n483:   only; a genuine mid-session model switch still applies forward, never\n484:   retroactively. The fields are **excluded from event identity** (adding them\n485:   to the id would make a rescan duplicate every pre-upgrade turn instead of\n486:   deduping it), and scanning is unaffected since `scan`/capture-tier\n487:   redaction walk only `content` and `raw.data`. *Forward-only, and\n488:   deliberately no backfill:* rewriting `producer` on stored events would keep\n489:   their ids but change their note lines, and under `cat_sort_uniq` the\n490:   rewritten and original lines both survive a merge with any peer still\n491:   holding the old one — two copies of one event. Old claude-code events lose\n492:   nothing permanent (their whole source line is in `raw.data`); old codex\n493:   events do, and re-capturing them does not help, since they dedup by id.\n494:   *Out of scope:* the rest of what those codex lines carry — `base_instructions`\n495:   (the full system prompt), sandbox/approval policy, reasoning effort — is\n496:   still not captured. That is a separate question (whole-session\n497:   configuration, not per-turn agent identity) with its own size and\n498:   redaction implications; this cut answers only \"what served this turn\".\n499: - **Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state** *(shipped,\n500:   0.10.0)* — Codex's `reasoning` response_items carry `encrypted_content`\n501:   that only OpenAI's servers can decrypt; the client just round-trips the\n502:   blob, and replaying it restores the model's hidden reasoning\n503:   provider-side. Capture previously dropped these outright, which conflicted\n504:   with the record-everything-just-in-case posture: we discarded them because\n505:   *we* can't read them, yet the one party who can (the provider) already saw\n506:   the plaintext. Realization (2026-07-21, turnbridge): dropping them\n507:   permanently forecloses restoring reasoning when a Codex-origin\n508:   conversation is fabricated back into Codex on another machine (or after a\n509:   codex→claude→codex round trip). Empirically verified same-account and\n510:   cross-account (turnbridge `scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs`): a real\n511:   blob replayed in a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI\n512:   versions, and even a different paid ChatGPT account with identical token\n513:   accounting — the ciphertext is not keyed per-account. Shipped shape: a new\n514:   `reasoning` kind, provider-agnostic (not Codex-specific), raw-only —\n515:   `content` carries only an opacity marker, the blob lives solely in `raw`.\n516:   Rides the ledger's normal default-on sync like every other kind (no special\n517:   carve-out). `cledger export` includes it by default (lossless dump);\n518:   `cledger log`/`show`/`conversations` hide it by default (`--with-reasoning`\n519:   to reveal) so ciphertext doesn't clutter human-facing transcripts. A\n520:   reasoning item's `summary` field (real, provider-decrypted plaintext when\n521:   reasoning-summary mode is on — distinct from `encrypted_content`) is split\n522:   out into an ordinary visible `conversation_turn` at the same seq, so it is\n523:   never hidden as if it were opaque. Capture-tier redaction and `cledger scan`\n524:   both exempt only the `encrypted_content` field from pattern-matching (it's\n525:   high-entropy by construction and this feature's entire point is preserving\n526:   it byte-exact — a coincidental rule match would silently corrupt it); every\n527:   other field, including `summary`, is scanned exactly like any other stored\n528:   content. Forward-only: sessions captured before 0.10.0 already had their\n529:   reasoning dropped and it's not recovered from what's on disk. *Remaining\n530:   open questions, mostly turnbridge's to answer at replay time:* blob TTL and\n531:   API-platform-org auth parity. *Deliberately out of scope here:* inter-agent\n532:   `agent_message` payloads still drop their embedded `encrypted_content`\n533:   blocks outright (same as before) rather than preserving them via this new\n534:   `reasoning` kind — same provider-withheld material, but embedded mid-line\n535:   rather than a standalone response_item, so preserving it needs its own\n536:   shape; tracked as a follow-up, not bundled into this cut. Also out of scope\n537:   at the time: no ledger event recorded which model/provider/CLI version\n538:   produced a given turn — a pre-existing gap in `Producer`, not specific to\n539:   reasoning, since closed in 0.12.0 (see the entry above). That gap mattered\n540:   most here: an encrypted blob is only replayable against the model that\n541:   produced it, so `reasoning` events captured before 0.12.0 carry ciphertext\n542:   with no record of where to send it.\n543: - **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n544: - **Forge (PR/MR) conversation adapter** — ingest pull-request review\n545:   discussions as ordinary conversation events anchored to the merge/squash\n546:   commit. The event schema already fits (a review comment is a visible turn\n547:   with a human actor); what doesn't slot into the existing adapter shape is\n548:   the capture surface — no local transcript file, no hook that fires on a\n549:   remote comment, needs API auth/pagination. The forge abstraction now\n550:   exists (`src/forge/`, built for re-anchor suggestions: PR-for-branch\n551:   lookup, GitHub driver over the user's `gh` session); this adapter would\n552:   extend it with comment fetching. GitLab/Gitea analogues exist for every\n553:   piece.\n554: - **Forge-side integration (CI / GitHub App)** — an opt-in Action/webhook\n555:   that runs where the squash actually happens: append the re-anchor mapping\n556:   event at merge time (no post-hoc detection needed), ingest the PR\n557:   conversation on merge, and push the notes ref from CI. Solves the\n558:   \"squashes happen off-machine\" problem at the source instead of\n559:   reconstructing it after fetch; kept separate from core because it requires\n560:   forge credentials and per-host setup.\n561: \n562: ## Storage model, in one paragraph\n563: \n564: One git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n565: note body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n566: content inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n567: conflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n568: repository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n569: capture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n570: rewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n571: for details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n572: tool deliberately refuses to become.\n573: \n574: ## License\n575: \n576: [MIT](LICENSE)\n\n(End of file - total 576 lines)\n</content>","status":"completed","time":{"end":1785583397810,"start":1785583397803},"title":"README.md"},"tool":"read","type":"tool"}},"format":"opencode-export-json/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T11:06:41.060Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"id":"deepseek-v4-flash","type":"agent"},"content":{"agent":"explore","blocks":[{"id":"call_6538e7c6695f00b5cf208c15c5bd495d","input":{"filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/src/adapters/codex.ts"},"name":"read","type":"tool_use"},{"content":"<path>/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/src/adapters/codex.ts</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: import { readFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\n2: import { mkdir, readFile, stat, writeFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n3: import { basename, join } from \"node:path\";\n4: import { findRepo, gitUserIdentity, type GitUserIdentity, type RepoInfo } from \"../git.js\";\n5: import { appendEvents } from \"../store.js\";\n6: import type { Actor, EventDraft, EvidenceEvent, ProducerAgentContext } from \"../schema.js\";\n7: import {\n8:   countUnrecognized,\n9:   reasoningDraft,\n10:   unrecognizedDraft,\n11:   warnUnrecognized,\n12:   type CaptureResult,\n13: } from \"./drift.js\";\n14: \n15: const RAW_FORMAT = \"codex-rollout-jsonl/2\";\n16: \n17: /** Codex CLI hooks-engine payload (subset we read). */\n18: interface CodexHookPayload {\n19:   session_id?: string;\n20:   transcript_path?: string;\n21:   cwd?: string;\n22:   hook_event_name?: string;\n23:   turn_id?: string;\n24: }\n25: \n26: /** One line of a `~/.codex/sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-*.jsonl` file. */\n27: interface CodexRolloutLine {\n28:   type?: string;\n29:   timestamp?: string;\n30:   payload?: Record<string, unknown>;\n31: }\n32: \n33: const CONVERTIBLE_RESPONSE_TYPES = new Set([\n34:   \"message\",\n35:   \"function_call\",\n36:   \"function_call_output\",\n37:   \"custom_tool_call\",\n38:   \"custom_tool_call_output\",\n39:   \"agent_message\",\n40: ]);\n41: \n42: /**\n43:  * Line types we deliberately do not capture: session/turn bookkeeping and\n44:  * the event_msg UI stream (whose conversation content duplicates\n45:  * response_item lines). Anything else is unrecognized — likely new upstream\n46:  * content — and counted for the drift warning; same for response_item\n47:  * payload types outside CONVERTIBLE_RESPONSE_TYPES. `reasoning` payloads are\n48:  * a third case: recognized, but their `encrypted_content` is opaque to us\n49:  * by design (only the provider can decrypt it), so they're captured as a\n50:  * `reasoning`-kind event instead of a `conversation_turn` or `unrecognized`\n51:  * one — see `reasoningDraft` and the capture loop below.\n52:  */\n53: const KNOWN_SKIPPED_LINE_TYPES = new Set([\n54:   \"session_meta\",\n55:   \"turn_context\",\n56:   \"compacted\",\n57:   \"event_msg\",\n58:   \"world_state\",\n59:   \"inter_agent_communication_metadata\",\n60: ]);\n61: \n62: /**\n63:  * Line types that state agent facts (model, provider, CLI version) about the\n64:  * response_items that follow them. They stay in KNOWN_SKIPPED_LINE_TYPES —\n65:  * they are still not captured as events of their own — but they are now read\n66:  * for `producer` metadata instead of being skipped outright.\n67:  */\n68: const CONTEXT_LINE_TYPES = new Set([\"session_meta\", \"turn_context\"]);\n69: \n70: /**\n71:  * Rolling agent context while scanning a rollout file. Unlike claude-code,\n72:  * codex does not stamp each response_item with the model that produced it:\n73:  * `session_meta` (once, at the top) states the CLI version and provider, and\n74:  * `turn_context` (once per turn, and re-emitted when the user switches model\n75:  * or effort mid-session) states the model. So the facts for a given line are\n76:  * whatever the most recent such line established — pure function of the file\n77:  * prefix, hence identical on every rescan.\n78:  */\n79: type CodexAgentState = ProducerAgentContext;\n80: \n81: /**\n82:  * Fold one line's agent facts into the rolling state. Fields are only ever\n83:  * overwritten by a later line that restates them, so a `turn_context` that\n84:  * omits `model` leaves the previous model standing (which is what codex\n85:  * means by omitting it).\n86:  */\n87: function applyContextLine(state: CodexAgentState, line: CodexRolloutLine): void {\n88:   const payload = line.payload;\n89:   // Callers reach this via a cheap substring pre-filter, which a conversation\n90:   // that merely talks *about* these line types also passes — so the type\n91:   // check here is load-bearing, not a formality.\n92:   if (!payload || typeof line.type !== \"string\" || !CONTEXT_LINE_TYPES.has(line.type)) return;\n93:   if (line.type === \"session_meta\") {\n94:     const cliVersion = payload[\"cli_version\"];\n95:     if (typeof cliVersion === \"string\" && cliVersion) state.source_version = cliVersion;\n96:     const provider = payload[\"model_provider\"];\n97:     if (typeof provider === \"string\" && provider) state.provider = provider;\n98:   }\n99:   // `model` is read from either line type: turn_context is where it normally\n100:   // lives, but session_meta has carried it in some codex versions.\n101:   const model = payload[\"model\"];\n102:   if (typeof model === \"string\" && model) state.model = model;\n103: }\n104: \n105: /** Parse a line only if it could plausibly be a context line. */\n106: function parseIfContextLine(text: string): CodexRolloutLine | null {\n107:   if (!text.includes('\"session_meta\"') && !text.includes('\"turn_context\"')) return null;\n108:   try {\n109:     return JSON.parse(text) as CodexRolloutLine;\n110:   } catch {\n111:     return null;\n112:   }\n113: }\n114: \n115: /**\n116:  * The agent context in force at `cursor`, i.e. the state the forward scan\n117:  * should start from.\n118:  *\n119:  * Two separate problems are solved here, both of which otherwise leave real\n120:  * events unlabelled:\n121:  *\n122:  * 1. *Resumed captures.* Incremental captures start mid-file, so a plain\n123:  *    forward scan would never see `session_meta` (line 0) and would miss every\n124:  *    `turn_context` from earlier turns — every event after the first batch\n125:  *    would lose its model. So the prefix is re-read. It costs only JSON\n126:  *    parsing of the few lines that could possibly be context lines: the whole\n127:  *    file is already read and split by the caller, and the substring guard\n128:  *    skips parsing for the overwhelming majority of them.\n129:  *\n130:  * 2. *Context lines that trail the items they describe.* Codex does not emit\n131:  *    `turn_context` before the turn's opening messages — in real rollouts it\n132:  *    lands a few lines in, after the first user message has already been\n133:  *    written. A strictly-forward rule would therefore leave the opening\n134:  *    message of every session with no model at all. Fields still unset after\n135:  *    the prefix scan are seeded from the *earliest* line that states them\n136:  *    anywhere ahead, which is not a guess: the first stated context is by\n137:  *    construction the first turn's, and the only items preceding it belong to\n138:  *    that same first turn. Later restatements never reach backwards — the\n139:  *    seed fills gaps only, and the forward scan overwrites it in file order.\n140:  */\n141: function initialAgentContext(lines: string[], cursor: number): CodexAgentState {\n142:   const state: CodexAgentState = {};\n143:   for (let i = 0; i < cursor && i < lines.length; i++) {\n144:     const line = parseIfContextLine(lines[i]!);\n145:     if (line) applyContextLine(state, line);\n146:   }\n147:   for (let i = cursor; i < lines.length; i++) {\n148:     if (state.source_version && state.provider && state.model) break;\n149:     const line = parseIfContextLine(lines[i]!);\n150:     if (!line) continue;\n151:     const stated: CodexAgentState = {};\n152:     applyContextLine(stated, line);\n153:     // First statement wins: `stated` is a fresh object per line, so an\n154:     // already-filled field is never clobbered by a later restatement.\n155:     if (!state.source_version && stated.source_version) state.source_version = stated.source_version;\n156:     if (!state.provider && stated.provider) state.provider = stated.provider;\n157:     if (!state.model && stated.model) state.model = stated.model;\n158:   }\n159:   return state;\n160: }\n161: \n162: function packageVersion(): string {\n163:   try {\n164:     const pkg = JSON.parse(\n165:       readFileSync(new URL(\"../../package.json\", import.meta.url), \"utf8\"),\n166:     ) as { version?: string };\n167:     return pkg.version ?? \"0.1.0\";\n168:   } catch {\n169:     return \"0.1.0\";\n170:   }\n171: }\n172: \n173: function sanitizeId(id: string): string {\n174:   return id.replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9_-]/g, \"_\");\n175: }\n176: \n177: function cursorPath(repo: RepoInfo, sessionId: string): string {\n178:   return join(repo.commonDir, \"conversation-ledger\", \"cursors\", `${sanitizeId(sessionId)}.json`);\n179: }\n180: \n181: async function readCursor(repo: RepoInfo, sessionId: string): Promise<number> {\n182:   try {\n183:     const raw = await readFile(cursorPath(repo, sessionId), \"utf8\");\n184:     const data = JSON.parse(raw) as { lines?: number };\n185:     return typeof data.lines === \"number\" ? data.lines : 0;\n186:   } catch {\n187:     return 0;\n188:   }\n189: }\n190: \n191: async function writeCursor(repo: RepoInfo, sessionId: string, lines: number): Promise<void> {\n192:   const path = cursorPath(repo, sessionId);\n193:   await mkdir(join(repo.commonDir, \"conversation-ledger\", \"cursors\"), { recursive: true });\n194:   await writeFile(path, JSON.stringify({ lines }) + \"\\n\");\n195: }\n196: \n197: /** Extract the trailing uuid from `rollout-<ts>-<uuid>.jsonl`, else the bare filename. */\n198: function sessionIdFromFilename(transcriptPath: string): string {\n199:   const name = basename(transcriptPath, \".jsonl\");\n200:   const match = name.match(/rollout-.*-([0-9a-fA-F-]{36})$/);\n201:   return match?.[1] ?? name;\n202: }\n203: \n204: /** `rollout-YYYY-MM-DDThh-mm-ss-*.jsonl` timestamp, else file mtime, else now. */\n205: async function sessionB